+\paragraph{General finite logarithmic recovery (Block 20).}
+Let $A$ be a rational algebra, without any commutativity assumption on $A$.
+For a unipotent element $U$ and a positive integer $d$ satisfying
+$(U-1)^d=0$, put
+\[
+L_d(U)=\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}{j}(U-1)^j.
+\]
+The value is independent of the chosen annihilating exponent. The finite
+logarithm and the nilpotent exponential satisfy
+\[
+L(\exp N)=N,\qquad \exp(L(U))=U.
+\]
+Both maps preserve every annihilating exponent, and hence preserve the
+exact nilpotency class of $N$ and $\exp(N)-1$, or of $U-1$ and $L(U)$.
+The Lean finite sum includes its zero-index term with coefficient zero;
+the displayed mathematical sum starts at one. The formal statements also
+handle the zero algebra and the exponent-zero case.
+
+The proof evaluates Mathlib's formal power-series inverse identities at
+constant nilpotent series. Such evaluation is exactly a finite polynomial.
+For an arbitrary noncommutative ambient algebra, perform the calculation
+inside the commutative subalgebra generated by the element and then apply
+its inclusion. No analytic convergence or topology is assumed. The
+finite logarithm commutes with ring homomorphisms between rational algebras.
+
+Let $K$ be a characteristic-zero field and $N$ an endomorphism with
+$N^d=0$. For $t\ne0$ the actual recovery formula is
+\[
+N=\frac1t\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}{j}
+       \bigl(\exp(tN)-1\bigr)^j.
+\]
+All coefficients lie in $K$. The vector spaces need not be finite
+dimensional, but nilpotence still means that some finite power of the
+whole operator is zero. A linear map intertwines two nilpotent operators exactly
+when it intertwines their exponential samples at a common nonzero
+parameter. Also
+\[
+\ker\bigl(\exp(tN)-1\bigr)=\ker N.
+\]
+
+For supplied covariance data and the twisted cocycle of Block 13, let
+$\rho_D(g)=\exp(t(g)N_D)r_D(g)$. If a common element $u$ satisfies
+$r_D(u)=r_E(u)=1$ and $t(u)\ne0$, every intertwiner of $\rho_D$ and
+$\rho_E$ recovers both the original $r$-intertwiner and the $N$-intertwiner.
+There is no cubic nilpotence restriction. This recovers equivariant
+projectors for both original components. Reconstructed-action isomorphisms
+induce actual isomorphisms of the inertia-fixed monodromy kernels. Their local characteristic polynomials
+agree under the existing finite-dimensional and normal-subgroup hypotheses.
+
+For actual finite original inertia images the common kernel sample is
+derived. Suppose $u\in H$ satisfies $\nu(u)=1$ and $t(u)\ne0$ and both
+$r_D(H)$ and $r_E(H)$ are finite. Their sampled actions have positive
+finite orders $a,b$. At $u^{ab}$ both original actions are trivial, and
+\[
+t(u^{ab})=(ab)t(u)\ne0
+\]
+by the cocycle identity and characteristic zero. Thus the same full
+intertwiner equivalence holds using the actual finite-image hypotheses,
+without a separately supplied common trivial-action sample. The initial
+nonzero cocycle sample and the arithmetic interpretation of the data
+remain requirements.
+
+Actual affine representations with monodromy chains of every finite
+length invoke these theorems. In particular a length-six example has
+$N^5\ne0$ and $N^6=0$ and is recovered from its actual reconstructed action.
+The length-four example still defeats the old quadratic logarithm, while
+the full finite logarithm recovers it.
+
+The sample hypotheses retain substantive content. A zero parameter loses
+$N$. For unrestricted supplied algebraic data, one can also absorb
